For every integer $n\ge1$, every convex body $K\subset\mathbb R^n$ satisfies the following bound, with equality if and only if $K$ is a simplex: $$\label{intro:simplex-bound}
 L_K\le
 \frac{(n!)^{1/n}}{(n+1)^{(n+1)/(2n)}\sqrt{n+2}}.$$

The functions in <a href="#tr:scalar-functions" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="tr:scalar-functions">[tr:scalar-functions]</a> satisfy, on $\mathbb R$, $$\label{tr:scalar-basic-equations}
 0<b<\frac12,\qquad l>0,\qquad
 \frac{d\log M}{d\log q}
 :=\frac{M'}{Md}\ge m_0.$$

Under <a href="#tr:smooth-assumptions" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="tr:smooth-assumptions">[tr:smooth-assumptions]</a>, put $J_r=\partial_rJ$ and $$\mathcal L u=\operatorname{tr}(J^{-1}D^2u)
             -\nabla V(\nabla\phi)\cdot\nabla u.$$ Then $$\label{tr:Jacobian-equation}
 \mathcal L J_{ij}
 =-\delta_{ij}+(JD^2V(\nabla\phi)J)_{ij}
   +\operatorname{tr}(J^{-1}J_iJ^{-1}J_j).$$ Moreover $\|\partial J\|<\infty$. The matrix fields $A$ and $k(A)$ belong to Gaussian $H^1$, and the integrations by parts below with smooth matrix functions of $J$ are justified by compact cutoffs.

For the transport satisfying <a href="#tr:smooth-assumptions" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="tr:smooth-assumptions">[tr:smooth-assumptions]</a>, with the notation above, $$\label{tr:surplus-inequality}
 \mathbb E\operatorname{tr}k(A)^2
 \ge\|\partial A\|^2-\|\partial k(A)\|^2+\mathcal C.$$

Define the symmetric $H^{-1}$ matrix field $$U=q(A)-(I+N)k(A)$$ and let $W$ be the Gram matrix of its rows in $H^{-1}$: $$W_{ij}=\sum_{\ell=1}^m
       \langle U_{i\ell},U_{j\ell}\rangle_{-1}.$$ Then $$\label{mat:covariance-completion}
 C=\mathbb E\bigl[J(RJ)\bigr]
   =W+\mathbb E\left(2t-k^2-\sum_r K_r^2\right),
 \qquad K_r=\partial_r k(A).$$ Moreover, if $U=u-\partial^*v$ with $u$ and the family $v=(v_r)_r$ square-integrable, then for every constant $S>0$, $$\label{mat:dual-bound}
 \mathop{\mathrm{tr}}(SW)\le\|u\|_S^2+\|v\|_S^2.$$

Let $a_i,a_j\in\mathbb R$ and $G,g,Z\in\mathbb R$. Set $$\delta=\bigl|\log(q(a_i)/q(a_j))\bigr|,\qquad
 r_{ij}=\frac{1+m_0(\min(a_i,a_j))}{16}
       \left(1+\frac{\delta^2}{30}+\frac{\delta^4}{1680}\right),$$ where $m_0$ is the six-interval lower bound in <a href="#tr:bins" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="tr:bins">[tr:bins]</a>. Define the endpoint residuals $$\begin{array}{lll}
 D_j=Gb_j+Z\Delta k_{ij},&L_j=Gl_j+Z\Delta b_{ij},&
 M_j=Ge_j+Z\psi_{ij}+cgb_j,\\
 D_i=Gb_i+Z\Delta k_{ji},&L_i=Gl_i+Z\Delta b_{ji},&
 M_i=Ge_i+Z\psi_{ji}+cgb_i.
 \end{array}$$ Let $Q(u,v)=\frac{43}{100}u^2+\frac{26}{100}uv+\frac{68}{25}v^2$. Then $$\begin{aligned}
 &\operatorname{avg}_{v=i,j}\left[
 s(D_v-\beta g)^2+\left(1+\frac s8\right)(D_v-\gamma g)^2
 +\gamma^2(s-1)g^2+\frac{L_v^2}{\alpha}
 +\frac{M_v^2}{4(1-\epsilon)p_v}\right]\notag\\
 &\hspace{15mm}\le Q(G,g-G/2)+r_{ij}\delta^2Z^2.
 \label{mat:pair-inequality}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $\operatorname{avg}_{v=i,j}$ is the arithmetic mean of the two endpoint expressions, counting both also when $i=j$. Moreover $p_v=p(a_v)>0$, and all divided differences extend continuously to $a_i=a_j$.

For every $a\in\mathbb R$, $$\label{sc:p-h-bounds}
 \begin{gathered}
 p(a)>0,\qquad |h_0(a)|\le .67\sqrt{p(a)},\\
 p(a)\ge\begin{cases}(7+a^2)^{-1},&a\le0,\\ .25,&a\ge0,\end{cases}
 \qquad p(a)\ge .46\quad(a\ge4).
 \end{gathered}$$ The following interval bounds hold: $$\label{sc:interval-table}
\begin{array}{c|cccccc}
 &b&\mathsf L\le&\mathsf D\le&K_0&J_0\le&\ell_1\\\hline
 O &[0,.09]&.60&.295(7+a^2)&[.37,.56]&.72&.13\\
 P_1 &[.083,.15]&1.01&4.3&[.29,.46]&.72&.13\\
 R_1 &[.14,.23]&1.19&3.00&[.29,.371]&.68&.13\\
 U_1 &[.22,.342]&1.19&1.72&[.29,.398]&.61&.123\\
 V_1 &[.337,.45]&.83&.87&[.31,.4]&.50&.060\\
 T_1 &[.445,.5]&.20&.640&[.32,.4]&.48&.022
\end{array}$$ Here $\ell_1$ is the positive number in the last column, and $$\label{sc:local-surplus}
 Q-
 \begin{pmatrix}
 A_*b^2-\eta_*b+\nu_*/4+l^2/\alpha&\nu_*/2-\eta_*b\\
 \nu_*/2-\eta_*b&\nu_*
 \end{pmatrix}
 -\mathsf D
 \begin{pmatrix}\mathsf E\\cb\end{pmatrix}
 \begin{pmatrix}\mathsf E&cb\end{pmatrix}
 \succeq\begin{pmatrix}\ell_1&0\\0&.75\end{pmatrix}.$$ Here $Q=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}.43&.13\\.13&2.72\end{smallmatrix}\right)$, as in Proposition <a href="#mat:scalar-pair" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="mat:scalar-pair">13</a>. Finally, writing $$\label{sc:derivative-functions}
 z=\frac{l'}{l},\qquad h_*=1+3b+kz,\qquad \chi=\frac{l}{d},$$ we have $$\label{sc:derivative-table}
\begin{array}{c|ccc}
 &z&h_*&\chi\le\\\hline
 a\le-1&[-.08,.70]&[.8,1.77]&.070\\
 a\ge-1&[-.475,0]&[.8,1.77]&.097
\end{array}$$ At an endpoint shared by two intervals, either applicable row may be used.

Let $f=e^{-V}$ be a log-concave probability density on $\mathbb R^m$, represented by a proper lower semicontinuous convex potential $V$. There are smooth convex potentials $V_j$ and probability densities $$f_j=Z_j^{-1}e^{-V_j},\qquad Z_j=\int_{\mathbb R^m}e^{-V_j},$$ such that $$\label{geo:hessian-bounds}
  \frac2jI\preceq D^2V_j\preceq\left(j+\frac2j\right)I
  \qquad(j\geq1),$$ and $$\label{geo:approximation-limits}
  Z_j\longrightarrow1,\qquad \lVert f_j-f\rVert_{L^1}\longrightarrow0,\qquad
  h(f_j)\longrightarrow h(f),\qquad
  \mathop{\mathrm{Cov}}(f_j)\longrightarrow\mathop{\mathrm{Cov}}(f).$$ The covariance limit is entrywise, the means of $f_j$ converge to the mean of $f$, and all these quantities are finite.
